<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cnut the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 990–1035</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Canute the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cnut the Great was a Danish prince, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a>, who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1016 and became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1018 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1028. Ruling this North Sea empire largely from England, he kept English law and institutions, used Danish and English earls and patronised the Church. His empire broke up soon after his death in 1035.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great.jpg" alt="Cnut the Great" width="296" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See Description (unknown) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canute_and_%C3%86lfgifu_cropped_(Canute).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cnut was a son of Swein Forkbeard and came from a line of Scandinavian rulers central to the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a> was king at the time of the Christianization of Denmark. Neither the place nor the date of his birth is known. He was already a Christian before he was king, and was named Lambert at his baptism. In September 1015 he landed at Sandwich, sailed round Kent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and harried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiltshire">Wiltshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, beginning a campaign of an intensity not seen since the days of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 18 October 1016 Cnut's army defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a>'s at the Battle of Assandun in Essex, where Eadric Streona withdrew his forces and brought about a decisive English defeat. Cnut ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for nearly two decades. He quickly eliminated possible challengers from the Wessex dynasty: Eadwig Ætheling was killed on his orders, and Edmund Ironside's sons fled abroad. In July 1017 he married Queen Emma, widow of Æthelred. In 1018 he collected a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danegeld">Danegeld</a> of £72,000, with another £10,500 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, paid off his army and kept 40 ships as a standing force.</p><p>When Harald II died in 1018, Cnut went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to secure the throne. At Easter 1027 he attended the coronation of the Holy Roman Emperor Conrad II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where he sought lower costs for the pallium of English archbishops and better conditions for pilgrims. On the way back he wrote that he was king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Denmark and the Norwegians and of some of the Swedes. He claimed the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a> in 1028. He repaired churches and monasteries that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids had plundered and was a patron of monastic communities, though his marriage to Emma while he was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelfgifu-of-northampton">Ælfgifu of Northampton</a> conflicted with Church teaching.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cnut died on 12 November 1035 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> and was buried in the Old Minster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a>, his son by Ælfgifu, became regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and claimed the throne in 1037. The medieval historian Norman Cantor called Cnut the most effective king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> history. Henry of Huntingdon, in the early twelfth century, first recorded the story of Cnut commanding the tide not to wet him, which has become the best-known story about him. In modern readings he is usually a wise man who knew from the start that he could not control the waves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cnut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sweyn’s raids on England culminated in its conquest in 1013, and his son <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England from 1016 and Denmark from about 1018, adding Norway in 1028.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Viking kings, the last of them Eric Bloodaxe, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under Æthelred the Unready renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013 and by his son <b>Cnut</b> in 1016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Driven out by <b>Cnut the Great</b>, Olaf Haraldsson returned and was killed at the Battle of Stiklestad in 1030, and his rapid veneration as Saint Olaf gave the monarchy a sacred patron and a lasting focus of loyalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A second wave of attacks from the 980s brought heavy tributes, the Danegeld, and the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013; his son <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England, the Kingdom of Denmark and, from 1028, the Kingdom of Norway.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>And at Roskilde, at Christmas 1026, <b>Cnut the Great</b> had his regent and brother-in-law Ulf Jarl killed after a quarrel over chess.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings kept armed retinues, called out the ship levy (leiðangr) and spread the new faith, sometimes by force, and <b>Cnut the Great</b> ruled England, Denmark and Norway together.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaftesbury">Shaftesbury</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=1019" aria-label="Denmark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4767 256 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cnut the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5217 526h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5217 526h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56,10&amp;year=1019">What was here in 1019? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I have vowed to God himself, henceforward to reform my life in all things, and justly, and piously to govern the kingdoms and the people subject to me, and to maintain equal justice in all things…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the English from Rome, 1027 (William of Malmesbury, Chronicle of the Kings of England, book 2) &#183; tr. John Sharpe, 1815, revised by J. A. Giles, 1847 &#183; the letter as William of Malmesbury copied it a century later &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/50778/pg50778-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that they by no means, either through fear of myself, or favour to any powerful person, suffer, henceforth, any injustice, or cause such, to be done in all my kingdom.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the English from Rome, 1027 (William of Malmesbury, Chronicle of the Kings of England, book 2) &#183; tr. John Sharpe, 1815, revised by J. A. Giles, 1847 &#183; the letter as William of Malmesbury copied it a century later; to his counsellors &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/50778/pg50778-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
